      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784681837339,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Chp 8 Flash Attention: resolves High bandwidth memory bottleneck",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Chp 8 Flash Attention: resolves High bandwidth memory bottleneck",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "c_fbt_8fVvEQidkuyHJ29",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -615.4683282553193,
      "y": 27.412725514483668,
      "width": 1786.5639648437505,
      "height": 800,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "KNS5n0fWJIHhRf6mcOM8j",
      "index": "a5V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1127293640,
      "version": 1594,
      "versionNonce": 2067371464,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784682521049,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "8.2 Designing Flash Attention\nNow understanding standard attention is HBM bound between i/o,\n    The core ideas are tiling (processing the attention matrix in blocks that fit in SRAM)\n    and online softmax (maaintaining running statistics so we never need the full row of scores at once)\n\n8.2.2 (revisit 8.2.1) The flash attention solution: tiling and online softmax\n    -> restructing the computation to avoid writing large N x N to HBM\n    instead of computing at once, we process in smaller blocks or tiles to fit in GPU SRAM\n        ** computing the output of softmax requires global information to HBM **\n    \"sliding window\" we can compute softmax iteratively, maintaining running statistics that allow processing one tile at a tile while producing mathematically exact results\n\n8.2.3 Tiling: breaking the problem down\n    -> break down the large, Q, K, and V matrices into smaller blocks then compute attention block by block loading only necessary tiles of Q, K, V into SRAM\n    -> for each block output matrix (O), iterate of K and V matrices to accumulate the results\n\n8.2.4 Online softmax: computing global statistics iteratively\n    -> the softmax requires the sum over all elements in a row to normalize\n\nHere’s how it works. As we process each tile of scores, we maintain two running statistics in registers or SRAM: a running maximum m and a running sum of exponentials l. When we\nprocess the first tile and compute scores for columns 0 through 127, we find the maximum value in those scores and store it as m. We compute the sum of exponentials\nsum(exp(score - m)) and store it as l. Now comes the critical part. When we process the second tile with scores for columns 128 through 255, we might find a new, larger maximum\nvalue. Let’s call this new local maximum m_new. This new maximum might be larger than our running maximum m.\n\nIf the new maximum is larger, we face a problem. All our previous exponentials were computed relative to the old maximum m. To maintain mathematical correctness, we need to\n\"rescale\" our previous running sum to be consistent with the new maximum m_new. The correction factor is exp(m_old - m_new). We multiply our running sum l by this correction factor,\nthen add the new partial sum computed with the new maximum. This ensures that when we finally divide by l at the end, we get exactly the same result as if we had computed the\nsoftmax over the entire row at once. The algorithm is not an approximation. It produces bit-for-bit identical results to standard softmax, but it never materializes the full row of\nscores in memory.\n\nThis iterative calculation is what makes Flash Attention mathematically valid.\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "8.2 Designing Flash Attention\nNow understanding standard attention is HBM bound between i/o,\n    The core ideas are tiling (processing the attention matrix in blocks that fit in SRAM)\n    and online softmax (maaintaining running statistics so we never need the full row of scores at once)\n\n8.2.2 (revisit 8.2.1) The flash attention solution: tiling and online softmax\n    -> restructing the computation to avoid writing large N x N to HBM\n    instead of computing at once, we process in smaller blocks or tiles to fit in GPU SRAM\n        ** computing the output of softmax requires global information to HBM **\n    \"sliding window\" we can compute softmax iteratively, maintaining running statistics that allow processing one tile at a tile while producing mathematically exact results\n\n8.2.3 Tiling: breaking the problem down\n    -> break down the large, Q, K, and V matrices into smaller blocks then compute attention block by block loading only necessary tiles of Q, K, V into SRAM\n    -> for each block output matrix (O), iterate of K and V matrices to accumulate the results\n\n8.2.4 Online softmax: computing global statistics iteratively\n    -> the softmax requires the sum over all elements in a row to normalize\n\nHere’s how it works. As we process each tile of scores, we maintain two running statistics in registers or SRAM: a running maximum m and a running sum of exponentials l. When we process the first tile and compute scores for columns 0 through 127, we find the maximum value in those scores and store it as m. We compute the sum of exponentials sum(exp(score - m)) and store it as l. Now comes the critical part. When we process the second tile with scores for columns 128 through 255, we might find a new, larger maximum value. Let’s call this new local maximum m_new. This new maximum might be larger than our running maximum m.\n\nIf the new maximum is larger, we face a problem. All our previous exponentials were computed relative to the old maximum m. To maintain mathematical correctness, we need to \"rescale\" our previous running sum to be consistent with the new maximum m_new. The correction factor is exp(m_old - m_new). We multiply our running sum l by this correction factor, then add the new partial sum computed with the new maximum. This ensures that when we finally divide by l at the end, we get exactly the same result as if we had computed the softmax over the entire row at once. The algorithm is not an approximation. It produces bit-for-bit identical results to standard softmax, but it never materializes the full row of scores in memory.\n\nThis iterative calculation is what makes Flash Attention mathematically valid.\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
